#
# Normalise line endings so a checkout on any platform is byte-identical.
#
# Without this file, Git applies no normalisation and a Windows checkout with
# the Git-for-Windows default core.autocrlf=true materialises every text file
# with CRLF. `gofmt` accepts only LF, so the Windows CI legs reported
# LF-only sources as unformatted and failed before tests ever ran. The defect
# was in the repository's line-ending policy, not in the Go sources.
#
# text=auto lets Git detect text files and store LF in the object database;
# eol=lf forces LF in the working tree on every platform, including Windows.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Go sources are always LF. gofmt rejects CRLF, and go:generate tooling and
# golden-file comparisons are byte-sensitive.
*.go text eol=lf

# Shell scripts, Makefile and CI configuration must keep LF: a CRLF shell
# script fails to execute, and the Makefile depends on tab indentation.
*.sh text eol=lf
Makefile text eol=lf
*.yml text eol=lf
*.yaml text eol=lf
*.mk text eol=lf

# Documentation and data.
*.md text eol=lf
*.mod text eol=lf
*.sum text eol=lf
NOTICE text eol=lf

# Binary assets must never be normalised. The authoritative specification PDF
# is untracked and ignored, but declaring the class keeps a future committed
# binary from being silently rewritten by a line-ending conversion.
*.pdf binary
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
*.zip binary
*.gz binary
